  <Placemark>
    <name>Waddell-Dudensing-Bering Home</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Address:</b> 5323 Post Oak Point Rd., New Ulm<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> F.B. Waddell built the front portion and loft of this house about 1842. When German-born William Dudensing (d. 1892) bought the property in 1873, he added rooms at the back. The porch was extended across the front by others in his family who owned the house until 1956. August C. Bering, III, a descendant of Germans who emigrated to Texas in 1846, purchased (1968), enlarged, and restored the structure. Staircase features original stenciled trim. Recorded Texas Historic Landmark - 1974]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-96.5264031523941,29.9285291038401,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>William Shelburne Cemetery</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[1.7 mi. W of Nelsonville on SH 159, then S on Zettle Rd.; cem. is .2 mi. SW of Zettle Rd.<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Nelsonville<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> William Shelburne Cemetery Established 1878 Historic Texas Cemetery - 2002]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-96.4337252915014,29.9675083226973,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Witte-Schmid Cemetery</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Address:</b> 2650 Witte-Schmid Road, Shelby<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> This pioneer graveyard is an important reminder of the German heritage of Austin county. Following two of their sons to America, Dr. Ernst Witte (b. 1791), a native of Blomberg, Germany, and his wife Lizette (Linnemann) bought 1422 acres here in 1856. Near this site, the Wittes built a home which Witte heirs later donated to the Texas German Society. Dr. Witte died in 1869 and was buried here; Lizette died in 1883. The following year, the Wittes' heirs sold the land to Ernst and Lizette's granddaughter Emilie Sophia and her husband John Scmid. More than twenty family members are buried here. The cemetery features upright marble and granite gravestones, with some inscriptions written in German.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-96.558517,30.000106,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Witte-Schmid House</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[Witte-Schmid Road, northeast of Schoenau Road and FM 1457<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> 2650 Witte-Schmid Road, Shelby<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Dr. Ernst Witte, educated as a lawyer in Hanover, Germany, prior to immigrating to Texas, purchased 1,422 acres of land in 1856, at the age of 63, and quickly improved the property value, producing cotton and adding this farmhouse around 1860. Witte died in 1869, leaving the property to his widow, Lisette. In 1884, her heirs sold the house to Emilie, grandchild to Ernst and Lisette, and John Schmid, a Swiss settler, where it remained in the Schmid family until 1986. An excellent example of rural vernacular architecture, the house was constructed with stuccoed sandstone, with interior walls of fachwerk and locally-sourced adobe. It includes a center-passage plan, asymmetrical placement of the hall with two summer beams and flat ornamental balusters, common characteristics of homes built by successful German settlers in the area in the mid-19th century. On the exterior, two of the most conspicuous Germanic details are the clipped gables, or schiefergedecktes dach, and the eyebrow windows (removed in the 1960s and restored in 2004), or dachgaube. These features were apparently inspired by Witte’s Home in Kleefeld, near Hanover. Relatively unchanged, the porch was added around 1910 by the Schmid family and the roof replaced in the 1960s and 2004. Overlooking the rolling landscape of Austin County, the Witte-Schmid house stands as a reminder of German immigration in the mid-19th century and its impact on the architectural history of Texas. Recorded Texas Historic Landmark - 2017]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-96.560719,30.001618,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
